body:
**Version Used**:  Microsoft (R) Build Engine version 16.5.0+d4cbfca49 for .NET Core (I was unable to google where I can get roslyn version if this is not it)

**Steps to Reproduce**:

```
class Program
    {
        public object? First;

        [NotNullIfNotNull(nameof(First))]
        public object? Second => First;

        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            var n = new Program();

            if (n.First is null) throw new ArgumentNullException();

            object f = n.First;
            object s = n.Second; // warning CS8600: Converting null literal or possible null value to non-nullable type.
        }
    }
```
**Expected Behavior**:
Neither `object f = n.First;` or `object s = n.Second;` gives out warning because first was explicitly checked and second has an attribute that it is null if first is not null.


**Actual Behavior**:
`object s = n.Second;` gives warning that I am assigning possible null value to a non-nullable variable.

**Related issues**:
Same attribute for operators
https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/issues/39802

Did some testing of s3 since I saw some issues trying to run `tpch` against the cloudformation node. Wrote a test as part of [#2976](https://github.com/xtdb/xtdb/issues/2976) that runs tpch at `0.05` scale, calling `finish-chunk!` after, noticed a few things:

-  This is enough to trigger a multipart upload - and the multipart upload is problematic.
-  Was trying to upload parts out of order, which caused issues - updated the way it handles part-numbers/sorting the list at the end to fix this, and pushed a fix to `2.x`
-  After fixing the above, started getting back `Your proposed upload is smaller than the minimum allowed size`  - seems like the parts it's trying to upload are smaller than 5MiB:

```
[{:part-number 1, :content-length 5256488}
 {:part-number 2, :content-length 5212824}
 {:part-number 3, :content-length 5176008}
 {:part-number 4, :content-length 5215192}
 {:part-number 5, :content-length 250578}]
```

We expect/are fine with the final part being <5MB (content length `5242880` ), but seems like some of the other parts are, too. Might be some slight issues with the way we're taking parts in the bufferpool.

This example code is incorrect:
https://github.com/Azure/azure-sdk-for-net/blob/master/sdk/storage/Azure.Storage.Queues/README.md#authenticating-with-azureidentity
``` c#
// Create a QueueClient that will authenticate through Active Directory
Uri accountUri = new Uri("https://MYSTORAGEACCOUNT.blob.core.windows.net/");
QueueClient queue = new QueueClient(accountUri, new DefaultAzureCredential());
```

It should be like:
``` c#
// Create a QueueClient that will authenticate through Active Directory
Uri accountUri = new Uri("https://MYSTORAGEACCOUNT.queue.core.windows.net/QUEUENAME");
QueueClient queue = new QueueClient(accountUri, new DefaultAzureCredential());
```

I reduced a complex failing method to a single for each loop, that can't be decompiled. Tested with latest git and any combination of openjdk8/11 and use-dx:

```java
import java.util.List;

public class LoopTest {
  public void testLoop(List<Object> objects) {
    for (Object o : objects) {
      if ((o.hashCode() != 42) || (o.hashCode() != 1)) {
        break;
      }
    }
  }
}
```

When performing an Ajax request, additional HTTP headers are discarded; in particular, the following code does not set the header `X-My-HTTP-Header` (checking in Chrome's Developer Tools, the header does not appear).
```js
$.ajax({
    method: "GET",
    url: "/example",
    headers: {"X-My-HTTP-Header": "HelloHeader"}
});
```


After some debugging, I discovered that the problem may be a wrong call to `$.each` in line 1874 of [this file](https://cdn.metroui.org.ua/v4/js/metro.js) (or, if I understand correctly the project building process, in line 1859 of [m4q.js](https://github.com/olton/Metro-UI-CSS/blob/master/source/m4q/m4q.js)). The particular portion of code is
```js
if (p.headers) {
    $.each(function(k, v){
        xhr.setRequestHeader(k, v);
        headers.push(k);
    });
}
```
Adding the missing `p.headers` in the `$.each` function call would solve the problem:
```js
if (p.headers) {
    $.each(p.headers, function(k, v){
        xhr.setRequestHeader(k, v);
        headers.push(k);
    });
}
```
